The pool is dominated by intermetallics (Heuslers, CsCl/AuCu3, NiAs, ThCr2Si2, etc.). These are metals (gap = 0) and are discarded. The remaining candidates were screened by oxidation-state balance, local coordination and known structural families to retain only those that must be insulators with a gap well above 1 eV.

Among the insulators the spin-orbit spillage is controlled by the heaviest atoms whose states form the band-edge manifolds (Bi 6p, Ir 5d, Hg 6s/5d, Tl 6p, Pt 5d). This ranking, combined with the gap filter, yields

- c048 (BiTe2Br4): molecular/vdW packing of heavy p-block atoms, large molecular-orbital SOC, gap of the constituent molecules \(\gtrsim 2\) eV;
- c046 (Ba4IrO6): isolated IrO6 octahedra, strong 5d SOC (possible \(j_{\rm eff}\) physics), experimentally insulating;
- c034 (In2HgO4): spinel, closed-shell Hg2+/In3+, optical gap analogous to CdIn2O4 but lowered by Hg 6s, still \(>1\) eV;
- c041 (TlBCl2): highly ionic cubic structure, Tl 6p conduction-band SOC, gap comparable to TlCl (\(\sim 3\) eV);
- c022 (PtS2): 1T-CdI2 structure, Pt4+ \(d^6\), established semiconductor gap 1.2–1.6 eV and sizable 5d SOC.

All other heavy-atom insulators either have mixed valence (gap collapse), are known to be \(\lt 1\) eV, or possess only moderate SOC.

Final: c048, c046, c034, c041, c022
